{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And behold, there came up out of the Nile seven cows, etc.</b> Since the health of the cows depends on the health of the creatures, for there is one pasture for the creatures and for the cows, and when the fodder fails for the animals in the field, it fails for man as well — for first, ״וְנָתַתִּי עֵשֶׂב בְּשָׂדְךָ לִבְהֶמְתֶּךָ״ (Deuteronomy 11:15), and afterward ״וְאָכַלְתָּ וְשָׂבָעְתָּ״ (Deuteronomy 11:15). And furthermore, if the animal is not healthy and good, who will plow with the cattle? And ״יְפוֹת מַרְאֶה״ may possibly refer to the eyes of the cows — that they had beautiful eyes — and this is a sign for the creatures, that their eyes would be fair one to another. For when one creature is hostile toward her fellow, the matter is visible in the eyes: if one sees one's fellow with laughing eyes or with glaring eyes — that is ״רָעוֹת מַרְאֶה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:44.520033201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}